  "result": "All 7 tests pass, including the 3 new ones in tests/test_t2.py.\n\n**What I changed** (only editor/buffer.py):\n\n- **Generalized the edit record.** The command object is now \"at `pos`, text `old` becomes `new`\" instead of separate insert/delete kinds. Insert has an empty old text, delete has an empty new text, and the inverse just swaps the two. Undo and redo logic is unchanged.\n- **Added `replace(pos, n, s)`.** It records one edit whose old text is the removed slice and whose new text is `s`, so it is a single undo step. It shares the delete method's bounds check, which I pulled into a helper.\n- **Added `history_len()`.** Returns the length of the undo stack.\n- **Added `undo_all()`.** Calls undo until it returns False, so every step lands on the redo stack in order and remains redoable.\n\nNothing under tests/ was touched, and the file remains standard-library only.",
